index 0000000..9addc60
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,132 @@
+# Kubernetes workshop: libvirt/KVM support
+
+This tree is the SpecterOps Kubernetes workshop with an additive libvirt/KVM
+provider. The existing VirtualBox and VMware Desktop configuration remains
+available.
+
+Read [ARCHITECTURE.md](ARCHITECTURE.md) before changing the network or synced
+folder configuration. The lab contains intentionally vulnerable services and a
+live Mythic C2 environment, so its isolation choices are deliberate.
+
+## Requirements
+
+- Vagrant 2.4 or newer
+- the `vagrant-libvirt` plugin
+- a working system libvirt/QEMU-KVM installation
+- sufficient capacity for four Ubuntu VMs (8 vCPUs and 16 GiB RAM in total)
+- one of the bidirectional synced-folder backends described below
+
+The default synced-folder setting is `auto`. It prefers virtiofs when
+`virtiofsd` is available, then an already-running NFS service, and finally 9p.
+It never selects rsync because the VMs pass generated cluster state through
+`/vagrant` in both directions.
+
+For libvirt, the Vagrantfile pins `bento/ubuntu-24.04` to `202508.03.0`, a
+release confirmed to publish a libvirt variant. The existing VirtualBox and
+VMware Desktop providers retain the upstream box-resolution behaviour. Override
+the libvirt pin with `K8S_LAB_BOX_VERSION` only after confirming the replacement
+publishes a libvirt variant.
+
+## Start the lab
+
+Validate configuration before creating anything:
+
+```sh
+vagrant validate
+```
+
+Then start the complete lab:
+
+```sh
+vagrant up --no-parallel --provider=libvirt
+```
+
+The Vagrantfile also disables parallel startup automatically. The explicit flag
+documents the requirement: workers and the teamserver consume artefacts written
+by the control plane.
+
+## Preserved lab topology
+
+The functional topology shown in the course diagram is preserved:
+
+| Diagram element | Libvirt conversion |
+| --- | --- |
+| Teamserver | `192.168.56.10`, DNS `teamserver` |
+| Control plane | `192.168.56.20`, API server on TCP 6443 |
+| Worker 1 | `192.168.56.30` |
+| Worker 2 | `192.168.56.31` |
+| Mythic UI | TCP 7443 |
+| Callback port | TCP 8081 |
+| TLS registry | TCP 5000, trusted by the cluster nodes |
+| Mythic callbacks | Developer and noaccess callbacks retained |
+| Host access | Vagrant forwards ports 7443 and 8081 to the teamserver |
+| Private lab traffic | Remains on the isolated `192.168.56.0/24` network |
+| Internet access | Retained through adapter 1 |
+| Hostname resolution | Retained across all four VMs |
+| Code-server and lab manifests | Unchanged from upstream |
+
+There is one deliberate implementation difference:
+
+- VirtualBox gives each VM an independent NAT engine on adapter 1.
+- Libvirt connects all four VMs to the dedicated `k8s-workshop-mgmt` NAT
+  network, normally `192.168.157.0/24`.
+
+The libvirt VMs can therefore communicate through adapter 1, whereas
+VirtualBox's separate NAT adapters do not provide that path. Kubernetes, Calico,
+Mythic callbacks, the registry and lab hostname resolution are explicitly pinned
+to adapter 2, so the lab topology remains unchanged.
+
+Mythic is available directly at `https://192.168.56.10:7443`. Forwarded host
+ports 7443 and 8081 remain conveniences and may be auto-corrected if occupied.
+
+## Host-specific overrides
+
+If the preflight reports an overlapping route or libvirt network, select unused
+private `/24` ranges:
+
+```sh
+K8S_LAB_MGMT_NETWORK=172.28.240.0/24 \
+K8S_LAB_PRIVATE_NETWORK=192.168.77.0/24 \
+vagrant up --no-parallel --provider=libvirt
+```
+
+Keep the same values for every later Vagrant command against that environment.
+The node host numbers remain `.10`, `.20`, `.30`, and `.31`.
+
+Select a synced-folder backend explicitly when required:
+
+```sh
+K8S_LAB_SYNCED_FOLDER=9p vagrant up --provider=libvirt
+K8S_LAB_SYNCED_FOLDER=virtiofs vagrant up --provider=libvirt
+K8S_LAB_SYNCED_FOLDER=nfs vagrant up --provider=libvirt
+```
+
+Virtiofs requires a recent libvirt/QEMU stack and a separately installed
+`virtiofsd`. NFS is configured as NFSv4 over TCP and requires an operational host
+NFS service plus the `exportfs` command. Backend discovery does not assume
+systemd: NFS is considered active when its local TCP service accepts connections
+on port 2049. Explicit backend availability is checked by the libvirt preflight,
+so loading this Vagrantfile for VirtualBox or VMware does not require libvirt host
+tools. 9p needs no host daemon but is slower.
+
+## Container registry rate limits
+
+The upstream provisioning scripts anonymously pre-pull the Calico and Tigera
+container images from Docker Hub inside each Kubernetes VM. Docker Hub can return
+HTTP 429 when its anonymous pull quota is exhausted; this is an in-guest
+containerd image pull, not use of Docker as the Vagrant provider.
+
+If this happens, wait for the quota to reset or configure authenticated/mirrored
+Docker Hub access for containerd inside the guests, then reprovision the failed
+machine. A Docker login on the host alone does not authenticate containerd inside
+the VMs. Pre-loading the required images into the `k8s.io` containerd namespace is
+also sufficient before reprovisioning.
+
+## Teardown
+
+```sh
+vagrant destroy -f
+```
+
+Generated credentials and state are ignored by Git. Review their paths before
+removing them; do not use a broad recursive cleanup command in the project root.
